<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wilhelm II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">abdicated <a href="/years/1918">1918</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-hohenzollern">House of Hohenzollern</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wilhelm II was the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-emperor">German Emperor</a> and King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, reigning from 1888 until his abdication in 1918. After dismissing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bismarck-battleship">Bismarck</a> he pursued an assertive world policy, including a large naval programme that alienated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. His dismissal of Bismarck in 1890 exposed the system's fundamental weakness, and he abdicated on 9 November 1918 as revolution and defeat brought the empire to an end.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wilhelm-ii.jpg" alt="Wilhelm II" width="315" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Studio of Thomas Heinrich Voigt · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kaiser_Wilhelm_II_of_Germany_-_1902_(3x4_cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Wilhelm II</b>'s dismissal of Bismarck in 1890 exposed the system's fundamental weakness.</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-empire">German Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The country stayed neutral in the First World War, mobilising its army throughout and later granting asylum to the deposed <b>Kaiser Wilhelm II</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Kingdom of the Netherlands · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The conflict between Kruger’s government, the mining magnates, Cecil Rhodes and the British High Commissioner Alfred Milner drew the great powers into the region, and the telegram of congratulation that <b>Kaiser Wilhelm II</b> sent Kruger after the Jameson Raid strained Anglo-German relations.</blockquote><a href="/empire/south-african-republic">South African Republic · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>His son Frederick III reigned for only 99 days and was followed by <b>Wilhelm II</b>, whose frequent journeys led to a joke of the time that the title had become &quot;Heil dir im Sonderzug&quot; (&quot;Hail to thee in thy royal train&quot;).</blockquote><a href="/songs/heil-dir-im-siegerkranz">Heil dir im Siegerkranz</a></li><li><blockquote>It offered the ducal crown to <b>Wilhelm II</b>, and Germany recognised the duchy on 15 March.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-courland-and-semigallia-1918">Duchy of Courland and Semigallia (1918)</a></li><li><blockquote>On 9 November Philipp Scheidemann proclaimed a republic in Berlin as <b>William II</b> abdicated.</blockquote><a href="/state/weimar-republic">Weimar Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Kronprinzenpalais, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in Huis Doorn, in what is now <a href="/countries/netherlands">the Netherlands</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.52,13.4&amp;year=1918" aria-label="Kronprinzenpalais on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4851 352 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Wilhelm II"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5301 622h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5301 622h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.52,13.4&amp;year=1918">What was here in 1918? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
